			<tr>
				<td colspan="2"><span class="postbody">At a rough-hewn wooden table, in the dark corner of a small tavern, sat two people. One was a man, large and hard of body, his face young yet worn with years of battle. Scars crossed his hands, his light brown hair tied back in a tail. He wore a full suit of silver plate-mail, beneath the dirt and grime of travel one could see its expensive make, and imagine it shining in the sun’s bright light. On the table before him was a helm of the same make, and at his back was strapped a great two-handed broadsword, the like of which few blacksmiths could hope to make in their lifetime. An ancient and powerful weapon indeed, to look upon it, one knew they were looking at a great man of war.
<br />
	His companion was smaller, slim of body yet just as hard. Her long red hair, the color of an autumn maple’s leaf, hung free and shining down her back. She matched him in silver plate-mail, as if a set had been made for the two. Her’s, like his, fit her perfectly, as if a metal skin had grown over her. Looking on her, one could be fooled into thinking her simply a beauty dressed for show, but one look into her pale blue eyes broke that illusion. Her eyes were as hard as any seasoned mercenary, and both sides of her hung weapons, swords slim and fast, made to be swung in unison dealing double damage to an opponent. A look at her hands would show the calluses grown from long use of such weapons, and one would know they looked upon a rare woman, one raised and bred to the dance of battle. 
<br />
	They sat in companionable silence, eating a breakfast of bread, cheese and thick porridge. The early light of dawn was just beginning to break through the murky windows of the tavern, bringing in a few early-risers to break their fast.  Behind the counter a lone serving girl wiped glasses with a dingy cloth, she sighed and went into the kitchen to fetch more food. The new-comers glanced at the strangers in the corner, but it was too early to start trouble or even think much, so they were left alone. The woman glanced up from her meal at her male companion and shot him a quick smile, which was such a bright and happy thing that, for a moment, it completely transformed her face into that of a young woman in love, full of promise, hope and happiness. In turn he smiled back at her as he raised a cloth to wipe his face, pushing aside his empty bowl.
<br />
	“It was nice to spend our night in an inn for once.” She spoke softly, spooning up the last of her porridge. He nodded, leaning back in his chair, taking a quick glance around, a man ever-aware of his surroundings, on the knife-edge of action at all times. 
<br />
	“Indeed, and thank the Gods for a soft bed that did not squeak too much!” He grinned mischievously. The woman grinned back without even a blush at his ribald comment. A woman bred amongst men of the field was used to such comments, and much worse.
<br />
	“Well sir I’m glad you’ve enjoyed it, for it will soon be the hard ground again for us.” She wiped her mouth. “It will take us near a fortnight to reach the mountain city of Kaladim.”
<br />
	“Ah, it will be good to fight alongside the dwarves of Kaladim again. Honest, good people and amazing fighters. I always enjoy their company.” He nodded.
<br />
	“Yes, and this time we will be answering the King’s call against Ak’anon.” She shivered melodramatically and he smiled. “By the Gods, I dislike gnomes.”
<br />
	“Your swords eager for their blood, eh, M’Lady? Well you shall soon have it, and I’ll be glad of the coin for this venture, no matter who we fight.” 
<br />
	As they began to gather up their belongings from beneath the table, the door to the tavern swung open. The occupants glanced up, as tavern-patrons of small towns are wont to do, to assess the newcomer. A young half-elf stood in the door, dirty from hard travel, letting his eyes adjust to the dim light of the room.
<br />
	“Hail, Sir Elf.” The serving girl called from her counter. “What can I get ye?”
<br />
	“I am a messenger from Qeynos. I seek one who is named Sriani, who is said to have rested here this night.” The elf spoke, walking into the room. 
<br />
	The woman in the corner glanced at her companion with narrowed eyes.
<br />
	“What is this? A messenger, for me?”  She whispered. He shrugged.
<br />
	“Hail, Sir Elf.” Sriani called from her table, standing up to catch his attention. The elf switched his glance to her and visibly pailed. She was quite an imposing sight. “I am Sriani HeartofWolf, for whom you seek.”
<br />
	“Yes, you match the description I was given, but it hardly did you justice.” He made a leg to her and her male companion giggled. 
<br />
	“Oh ho, now you have an admirer.” He whispered. Sriani shot him a glare and he hid his smile behind his hand.
<br />
	The elf walked up to their table, pulling an envelope from his satchel. Sriani dug in her purse for a few coin, handing them to the messenger as he laid the envelope on the table. He bowed again.
<br />
	“I thankyou M’Lady. May the Gods bless you and your journeys.” He glanced over at the man and swallowed a lump in his throat. “Sir, I meant no offense to your lady, please forgive me.” And bowed again.
<br />
	“No offense taken, and the lady is not mine.” He laughed good-naturedly. “Not quite a lady at that. Get yerself on the road lad, you’ll find the women more biddable.”
<br />
	The elf blushed, bowed once more, and quickly walked out of the tavern.
<br />
	Sriani sat at the table, using a small knife that had been tucked behind the armor on her leg to cut open the envelope. She pulled out a few pages covered in tight, womanly letters, and began to read, her face becoming more concerned with every word. 
<br />
	“Chorun…” she whispered, and a seldom-seen expression crossed her face, one of worry and pain. Chorun reached a hand across the table to grasp at her’s.
<br />
	“What is it, my love?” He asked softly.
<br />
	“My mother…she writes begging me to return home at once.” Sriani glanced up, tears in her eyes. “My young sister has disappeared and none can find her.”
<br />
	Chorun frowned with concern of his own. 
<br />
	“Krisiana?” 
<br />
	Sriani nodded and angrily dashed a hand across her eyes to wipe away the tears.
<br />
	“I must go home, I cannot delay.” She said firmly, standing up and tucking the letter into one of her bags, gathering them up. She slung a few over her shoulder. Chorun stood with her and did the same.
<br />
	“Of course, and I go with you.” 
<br />
	“Chorun…you don’t have to. There’s money to be had with the king of the dwarves. Answer his call and earn yourself the coin, my love. There’s no reason you should miss this chance.” 
<br />
	“Indeed there is.” He reached out to place his hands on her shoulders, using the thumb of his left hand to prop her face up until she was looking into his eyes. “I made a promise long ago to follow you anywhere, to be your companion and husband. I will never abandon you, not for any amount of coin in the world.”
<br />
	She nodded, unlike many women she did not press the matter or fall apart into tears, she simply accepted his words and grabbed at his hand to squeeze it in her own.
<br />
	“Then we return home to Qeynos.” She said decisively. “And we leave at once.”
<br />
						***
<br />
	They rode hard and fast from the tavern, pushing their mounts to cover great lengths during the day, sleeping in snatches at night, only to rise before dawn and continue. Sriani’s worry drove them like a demon at their backs, and Chorun, true to his word, followed her, lending her his strength and stead-fast calm. It took only a week to cross the great plains of Karana, a journey that at a more sedate pace took most four weeks or more, if beset with bandits. If any bandits did think to worry them, they thought better of it seeing the look on her face, the look of a woman who would stop at nothing to pass. They waited for easier prey and let the two pass by unmolested.
<br />
	On the seventh morning since leaving the tiny un-named town outside of the great city of Freeport, Sriani and Chorun rode through the gates of Qeynos. It was a smaller city than the human home-city of Freeport, but it was its sister-city, a place friendly and home to humans, half-elves and a few sympathetic dwarves, Halflings and High Elves. The city was just beginning to wake for its day, the streets already filling with merchants, children at play, women shopping and men rushing to work. Guards in silver and red patrolled the streets, bowing low when they passed the two warriors. This was their home-town, and the people of Qeynos revered Sriani and Chorun as great heros, the tales of their deeds had traveled back quickly it seemed. No matter that neither had visited in many years, the people loved them dearly anyway. Cheers went up as they passed, children laughed and chased their horses, women tossed flowers from high windows, as if it were a festival day. 
<br />
	Chorun made sure to wave and smile to the people, but Sriani was focused on one purpose, her face hard with inner pain, and if anyone noticed they said nothing. 
<br />
They rode towards the back of the city, where lived her family in a tall house of three levels. Her father was a grower of flowers and herbs, and much-consulted by the city’s rulers for his knowledge of plants. His work could be seen all over Qeynos, for they had a deep love for growing things. Her mother was a great care-taker of the elderly, and a knowledgable mid-wife. She was famous for her skills, and called upon nearly daily to tend a sick grandmother, or oversee a girl’s first hard birth. Because of this, her parents lived in a home unlike most, it was tall and well-tended, as if the home of some rich merchant. It had its own private courtyard, where a young boy scampered up to take their horses.
<br />
He skidded to a stop in the dirt as he recognized them, and his face broke into a huge smile of delight.
<br />
	“Sriani!” He squealed, and ran full-tilt into her arms as she knelt down to accept him into open arms. They embraced for a long moment, her face against his cheek. “Sister, we did not expect you so soon.”
<br />
	“Zach, you have grown too much since I left.” She grinned, betraying none of her worry to her young brother. She pinched his cheek. “Driving the girls crazy yet, are you?”
<br />
	Zach blushed.
<br />
	“Naw, sister, the girls barely notice me. I’m too young yet.” 
<br />
	Sriani ruffled his hair as she rose. 
<br />
	“You’d be surprised, I’m sure they all giggle in secret about you.” 
<br />
	Zach laughed and took her hand.
<br />
	“Come in sister, come see mother, she’s waited for you every night, she’s hardly slept.” He dropped his voice in worry. “In truth I fear for her, she looks more ill every day Krisiana is gone.”
<br />
	Sriani glanced back at Chorun, who good-naturedly ignored the fact that he’d been left out of the welcome as he led their horses to the stables out back. He waved to her, a gesture that meant: “Go on in, I will follow shortly.”
<br />
	Zach led her through the front door into the cool inner chambers, and into the kitchen near the back of the house. 
<br />
	“Mother! Father! Sriani is home!” He cried with delight. The man and woman seated at the table turned quickly, their faces shining with joy. Sriani had to control an urge to break into tears. They both looked so old, their faces wrinkled where once they had been smooth, streaks of gray in their hair, and thin, oh so thin! When she had left them to find her fortune traveling with the Steel Warriors of Qeynos, her newly married husband, a young Paladin of Rodcet Nife at her side, they were  still young in her eyes, happy joyful parents in love with life and satisfied in their work, healthy and robust. Now…oh god now…. Sriani wanted to weep. What had her sister’s disappearance done to them? 
<br />
	They knew she could see the fear and worry engraved on their faces, and they stood to embrace her as one. She trembled, enveloped in her parent’s warm embrace, and they stood together for a long time, drinking in the comfort of being together again, saying nothing. Zach stood behind them, shifting from foot to foot, in the silence. 
<br />
	“Mother..father..” Sriani whispered. “It’s so good to see you again.”
<br />
	They broke apart, and her father smiled, holding her at arm’s length.
<br />
	“Oh what a woman you’ve become!” He laughed. “I am so glad to see you, look at you, you glow with health. The road has been good to you.”
<br />
	“Aye father.” She nodded, searching his face. Until this moment she had not thought how deeply she missed him, his steady hand and quick knowledge guiding her through her childhood. 
<br />
	“You’re taller than I remember.” Her mother spoke softly, reaching up to touch her daughter’s bright red hair. “And bless the Gods, not a scar on you.” 
<br />
	Sriani giggled then. 
<br />
	“Nay, but be sure I dealt some out.” 
<br />
	“Well, the Gods bless this day, sit down dear heart, let me fetch some food for you. And your husband, where is he?” Sriani’s mother guided her into a chair at the kitchen table. Her father sat across from her, taking up a piece of bread, munching it.
<br />
	“He’s tending our horses.” 
<br />
	She watched her mother move about the kitchen, bringing back memories of supper, all the happy times spent in this room, the center of their household. 
<br />
	“Tssk, he shouldn’t need to. Zach, have you gone and forgotten your duties? A guest should not be in the stables!” Her father admonished. Zach woke from whatever reverie had claimed him, standing by the table, and blushed.
<br />
	“Sorry, father. I was just so happy to see Sriani. I’ll go at once.” He ran from the kitchen and her father grinned.
<br />
	“He’s been such a help, truly a good boy. There’s a place for him in the Steel Warriors I’m sure.” He smiled with pride, thinking of having two of his children amongst that proud company. Sriani smiled back, though she knew the Steel was no place for her young brother. Looking at him and remembering their childhood together, she was sure his life would take another path, most likely into the scholarly studies of the Magician’s hall, where Enchanters, Mages and Wizards held council amongst their books and arcane magics. 
<br />
	“Father, let us not waste another moment. I have ridden hard and fast at your beckoning, for your letter scared me much. Please, tell me what is going on.” She said softly, loathe to break the joy of her homecoming, but anxious to know the details. 
<br />
	At that moment, Chorun entered the kitchen from the back door, and both her parents smiled at him as if he were their own son, the pride of the family. He kissed her mother on the cheek, and bent to embrace her father. 
<br />
	“It’s wonderful to see you both, Michaelle, Mari you both look wonderful.” He smiled, taking a seat beside Sriani. Sriani frowned, wondering if he simply missed how terrible they both looked or was simply hiding it.
<br />
	“Aye, I regret that this meeting has to come at the behest of such news.” Michaelle, her father, frowned. Mari sat down beside him and took his hand. “I should tell you the whole story I suppose, for it will make more sense for being told.”
<br />
	Sriani nodded, and Chorun took her hand under the table, lending his strength once more. Her father took a sip of water, coughed gently, and reached in his pocket for one of his ever-present hand-rolled cigars, lighting it and taking a slow puff while they all waited in tense silence for him to begin.
<br />
	“You know that your sister has always been…difficult.” He began with a sigh, glancing at his wife who gave him a sympathetic nod. “From the moment of her birth it seems her first words were ‘no’. Stubborn as a donkey, but we loved her, you know that. You know we gave her everything she needed or wanted…” His voice trembled, with anger or sadness, perhaps both.
<br />
	“I know father…please, continue.” Sriani said gently.
<br />
	“Well, just a year ago she began to start hanging out with some new crowd, I’d never met them before and certainly they’d never been her friends up until then. Ruffians and bad news, all, hanging at the docks after dark and starting trouble. We knew she was up to no good with them, she’d stumble in after dark smelling of cheap liquor, or the sweet smoke of the hajis-weed. She would argue with us daily, going into fits of temper…with nothing to start it but a word from her mother or me!”
<br />
	“She scared us, she was so violent!” Mari took up the story as her husband paused to sip water and puff on his cigar. “After the Festival of Thanks, she argued with us because we did not want her to go out that night, and she flew into a fit and hit me, so hard it was as if I were fighting with a bandit and not my own daughter! She screamed like a banshee, and flew out of the house and we did not see her for days.” Mari paused, glancing out the window for a moment. “She came home finally looking as if she had not eaten the entire time, dirty and delirous, she’d caught a fever somehow. She was sick for a week, and it seemed to calm her but…”
<br />
	“So how long has she been like this?” Sriani asked, wanting to hear the rest of the story.
<br />
	“A year or more, I’d say.” Michaelle said, and Mari nodded in confirmation.
<br />
	“Aye, I’d say that much. Ever since she met that crowd of hers, and then, oh the worst part Sriani, she met some…boy!” Mari frowned. “A little stink-thief he was, stealing her out at all hours, ignoring our pleas to visit or meet your father and me. Oh I know ‘twas not all his fault…Krisiana has a clever mind, that girl can spin a web ‘round anyone easy as a black spider, and I know she had him in her clutches!”
<br />
	“Bah, don’t defend him Mari! The boy was just so, a stink-thief, a rotten apple that I’d like to throw a few yards!” Michaelle spoke roughly. Mari laid a hand on his shoulder.
<br />
	“Calm yourself husband.”
<br />
	Sriani glanced from one to the other. 
<br />
	“So she’s taken off with him, am I to understand that?”
<br />
	“Well yes, we believe so. I won’t tell you anymore I suppose, just know that the last year has been a punishment for your father and I, dealing with that devil of a girl. We worried for her, worried so…she left her job at the milliner’s to loaf around with her man and his friends, and stopped attending the classes at the Temple. No amount of talking, arguing, shouting did any good! She just dug her feet into the ground and wouldn’t budge…no one could help us, the townsfolk are reluctant to involve themselves in anyones business, you know.”
<br />
	Sriani nodded wryly.
<br />
	“She even tried to make off with the boy once before, she came home one day saying she was off to live in his house with him and his mother! Can you imagine, the girl’s only 15! When I tried to stop her, she summoned the town guards on me, and they wouldn’t do a thing. Said she’s old enough to be an adult in her own right, just cuz she’s of marriable age, and they let her go off!” Michaelle was fairly shaking with rage. “She lived in that stink-hole of a house there by the docks for months! Wouldn’t let us see her, and wouldn’t step foot near the house if she could help it, aye, parading herself ‘round town like a little…”
<br />
	“Michaelle!” Mari frowned, interrupting him before he could speak any more ill. Sriani’s father paled, and looked down at his hands clenched together on the table. Sriani and Chorun shared a glance, worry plain on both their faces. 
<br />
	“Well, you get my meaning. I was right angry with that woman, too, letting my daughter live in her house, no job, no schooling, and not even a ring on her finger to proclaim them betrothed, which would have been a little better! Living in sin before the Gods they were, and laughing about it too! But I could do nothing…nothing…” Michaelle closed his eyes, took a deep breath. “I was powerless, bound by the laws and the Gods to stand by and watch my daughter turn away from the good life I made for her…into another parent’s home, living off them like a common street-rat, and having the audacity to slander mine and your mother’s name all over town.”
<br />
	“What? What did she say?” Sriani asked, concerned now by the terrible gravity of the events unfolding in her parent’s story.
<br />
	“That we had…” Michaelle paused, looked down at his hands again. “You know we’re good parents…we never laid a hand on any of ye…”
<br />
	“I know that, Father…” Sriani began, and then the hint of what he was trying to say struck her in full. “She said you hit her?”
<br />
	“Aye.” He gritted his teeth and refused to meet anyone’s eyes. Sriani could see he was shamed to tell this part of the tale, shamed above all things he was, and her heart ached for him. She did not want to see her strong, proud, steadfast father beaten down this way, prostrated by embarrassment.  She met Chorun’s eyes and saw her pain echoed in them, and knew he understood and shared her emotion. Under the table, their hands met. “Said I beat her like a barn dog, me and yer mother both.”
<br />
	“Filthy lies.” Zach spat from the corner, almost forgotten whilst everyone became wrapped up in the tale. “She’s always lied, like as if she can’t control her tongue and it’s possessed by some devil. I told her to her face I’d never look on her again if she told those lies about our parents. And I haven’t yet.”
<br />
	Sriani sighed and sat back in her chair, rubbing the tension from her forehead.
<br />
	“I’m sorry daughter, I do not mean to burden you so soon after your arrival. Come, let us forget this for a moment and take some time to catch up on happier news.” Mari reached across the table for Sriani’s hand, folding it in the rough warmth of her own.
<br />
	“No, now I am concerned and above all us I must find my sister, if not just to bring her safely home, but to bring her to heel for the deeds she has committed against you both.” Sriani spoke finally, her voice a knife-edge of rage. Chorun glanced at her with worry. He knew that voice, it was the voice she used when going into battle and fully accepting that she may die, but by the Gods she’d take a few down with her.
<br />
	“Sriani…” Mari begged, her voice soft and trembling. “You must remember she is sick, she is a sick girl and led astray by her youth…”
<br />
	“Mother, you and father may have to treat her gently because the Gods command it so, but I do not. I am her sister, not her mother. If she thinks for one minute she can walk all over the both of you and get away with it…well, rest assured this will not be so. I will find her, and I will bring her home by the ear if I must.” Sriani looked at both of them in turn, holding their gaze with the fiery strength of her own. “And she will kneel before you and beg for your mercy. This I swear.”<div style="vertical-align: bottom"><br /><br /><br />_________________<br /><a href="http://www.magelo.com/eq_view_profile.html?num=956867" target="_blank" class="postlink">~Sriani HeartofWolf~</a>
<br />
